Subject: [PATCH 0038] encoder: clear picked_ref_frames_mask only when it is used

init_encode_rd_sb() clears x->picked_ref_frames_mask once per superblock:

    av2_zero(x->picked_ref_frames_mask);

The array is MAX_MIB_SIZE * MAX_MIB_SIZE uint64 = 64 * 64 * 8 = 32,768
bytes. From callgrind on the current tip, __memset_avx2 reached through
init_encode_rd_sb is 0.53% of retired instructions at cpu-used=2.

BOTH ACCESSORS ARE GATED ON ONE SPEED FEATURE, THE CLEAR IS NOT

  * writer -- av2_update_picked_ref_frames_mask() is called from
    partition_search.c under

        if (cpi->sf.inter_sf.prune_ref_frames && xd->tree_type != CHROMA_PART)

  * reader -- fetch_picked_ref_frames_mask() is reached from rdopt.c under

        if (inter_sf->prune_ref_frames && !x->inter_mode_cache[0])

With inter_sf.prune_ref_frames == 0 the array is neither written nor read,
so clearing it is pure overhead. That is not a rare case: the flag is
initialised to 0, set conditionally in two places, and forced back to 0 in
two others, so several presets never use the array at all.

This patch guards the clear with the same flag.

WHY THIS IS BIT-EXACT

It skips a write to storage that, on the paths where the write is skipped,
has no reader -- the reader is behind the identical condition.

The flag is resolved per frame (one of its terms is the frame's boost
state), so the guard is re-evaluated for every superblock against the
current frame's speed features. A superblock in a frame where the flag is
on still clears the array at its own start, and nothing is carried over
from a frame where the clear was skipped: the array's only consumer reads
it only in frames where it was cleared and written.

Note the clear is already per-superblock rather than per-frame, so this
patch does not change the lifetime of the data, only whether the clear
happens at all.

SIZE

Smaller than the other memory-traffic patches in this series (0034 is
-2.67%, 0037 is -4.24%, 0036 is -0.42%), because it is per-superblock
rather than per-block. Included on the same reasoning as 0036: bit-exact,
a one-line guard, and a clean "written iff read" argument, so it costs
nothing to carry.

VERIFICATION

Bit-exactness by .obu md5 against the unpatched build over clip x preset x
qindex. The sweep spans QP 40-200 because prune_ref_frames has qindex- and
boost-dependent terms, uses 6-10 frame runs so both boosted and
non-boosted frames occur, and includes a --tune-content=screen run because
the flag also has an allow_screen_content_tools term.

MEASURING IT

Retired-instruction counts under callgrind, not wall clock. A change this
size is an order of magnitude below what local wall clock can resolve.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/encodeframe.c b/av2/encoder/encodeframe.c
index c20b6ba..21dd806 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/encodeframe.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/encodeframe.c
@@ -466,7 +466,19 @@ static INLINE void init_encode_rd_sb(AV2_COMP *cpi, ThreadData *td,
 
   // Reset hash state for transform/mode rd hash information
   reset_hash_records(&x->txfm_search_info);
-  av2_zero(x->picked_ref_frames_mask);
+  // picked_ref_frames_mask is MAX_MIB_SIZE^2 uint64 = 32 KB, cleared once per
+  // superblock. Both of its accessors are gated on the same speed feature:
+  // av2_update_picked_ref_frames_mask() writes it only under
+  // sf.inter_sf.prune_ref_frames (partition_search.c), and
+  // fetch_picked_ref_frames_mask() is reached only under the same flag
+  // (rdopt.c). With the flag off the array is neither written nor read, so
+  // clearing it is pure overhead -- and the flag is 0 by default and forced to
+  // 0 in several presets.
+  //
+  // The flag is resolved per frame, so a superblock in a frame where it is on
+  // still clears the array at its own start; nothing carries over from a frame
+  // where the clear was skipped.
+  if (sf->inter_sf.prune_ref_frames) av2_zero(x->picked_ref_frames_mask);
   av2_invalid_rd_stats(rd_cost);
   if (sf->part_sf.partition_search_type != VAR_BASED_PARTITION &&
       sf->part_sf.partition_search_type != FIXED_PARTITION) {
